## Toolchains

Install only what the project needs, and prefer the project's own version files.

- **Node, Python, Go, Ruby, Java, Bun, Deno, …**: use `mise`.
  - If the project declares versions (`mise.toml`, `.tool-versions`, `.nvmrc`,
    `.node-version`, `.python-version`, …), run `mise install`.
  - Otherwise install container-wide with `mise use -g node@22` (or the version the
    project's docs or `package.json` engines ask for). Do **not** add a
    `mise.toml` to the repository unless the user asks.
  - Then use the project's package manager as usual (`npm ci`, `pnpm install`,
    `pip install -r …`).
- **PHP**: use Debian packages (`sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y php-cli composer`)
  plus the extensions the project needs (`php-mbstring php-xml php-curl php-intl
  php-zip php-mysql php-sqlite3 php-gd …`). Check `composer.json` (`require`, `ext-*`).
- **Databases and services** (MariaDB, PostgreSQL, Redis, …): install with `apt`
  and run them inside the container if tests need them.

Report what you installed so the user can bake it into a project image later.

## Git

{{.GitText}}
{{if .IsWorktree}}
### Worktree

This is a linked git worktree of `{{.MainDir}}`, in a sandbox of its own: the main
checkout and the other worktrees are not here. The git directory is shared with
them, so branches, tags and stashes are too. Work on the branch checked out here;
do not delete, reset or rewrite other branches, and do not run `git worktree
prune`/`remove` or `git gc --prune=now`.
{{else if .WorktreesDir}}
### Worktrees

Parallel work lives in git worktrees under `{{.ProjectDir}}/{{.WorktreesDir}}/<name>`, one
branch each. The user opens them with `airlock wt`, each in its own Herdr workspace;
you can also make one:

    git worktree add {{.WorktreesDir}}/<name> -b <branch>

Work in the worktree you were started in, and leave the others alone. A worktree
may come with a task, which is added to your instructions when Pi starts in it.
Remove a worktree with `git worktree remove` when its work is merged or abandoned.
Some worktrees run in sandboxes of their own and are not visible here.
{{end}}
